# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
doubt	one
think	one
# A1 counts doubting as a kind of thinking. That fits the text's broad sense of "think": any conscious activity, including doubting. It is the same sense as "ceased to think" in P5, so A1 is not a bridge.
# C1's "whose whole essence consists only in thinking" may turn thinking from an activity into a defining nature. But C1 is not among the shared claims, so no line is given for it here.
